       			STRING BUFFER : 5.1)It is completely thread-safe. Multiple threads can access and modify it at the same time without causing any data corruption.	
       							5.2)Every major method is synchronized. This means Java automatically locks the object when a thread uses it, forcing other threads to wait their turn.			
       							5.3)It is slower in execution speed. The system overhead required to manage, acquire, and release thread locks slows down its processing time.	
       							5.4) This is a legacy class. It has been a part of the core Java framework since the original Java 1.0 release.
       							5.5) Use this only if you are working on legacy code or if a single mutable string must be actively modified by multiple threads at once.						           
       		   STRING BUILDER : A.1)It is not thread-safe. It is unsafe to use when multiple threads are accessing and modifying it simultaneously.
       		   					A.2) Methods are not synchronized. There are no object locks applied during execution, allowing open access.
       		   					A.3)It is much faster and more efficient. Because it completely skips the lock management system, it executes string operations instantly.
       		   					A.4)This is a modern alternative. It was introduced later in Java 1.5 to provide a high-performance choice for single-threaded tasks.
       		   					A.5)Use this by default for 99% of your standard Java programs, loops, local variables, and coding logic where speed is vital.					